[h1]Enshrouded Sleep[/h1]

Let some players sleep through the night without waiting for everyone else. For Project Zomboid Build 42 dedicated servers.

[b]Tested with:[/b] Project Zomboid 42.21.0

[h2]Why your server needs it[/h2]
In vanilla multiplayer, time only speeds up when every living player is asleep. On a server where people play on different schedules that rarely happens, so a player who goes to bed sits through the whole night in real time.

[h2]How it works[/h2]
- When some players are asleep, world time speeds up in proportion to how many are sleeping. With half the players asleep, time runs at about half of vanilla's full-sleep speed.
- Only the clock and calendar move faster. Awake players keep moving, fighting, driving, and crafting at normal speed.
- Awake players are protected from the faster clock: their hunger, thirst, fatigue, and nutrition progress at the normal rate. Sleeping players are left to vanilla.
- When everyone is asleep, the mod steps aside and vanilla's full-sleep fast-forward takes over. When everyone is awake, time runs at your server's normal day length.
- The server decides the speed and keeps every client's clock in step, so clocks don't jump.

[h2]Things to know[/h2]
- Anything else that runs on game time moves faster during partial sleep, just as it does during vanilla sleep: food spoilage, generator fuel, crops, weather, corpses, and other mods' timers.
- Dedicated servers only. Single-player is not supported.

[h2]Server setup[/h2]
Back up your world and server configuration before installing or updating.

[code]
WorkshopItems=3786842301
Mods=pz-enshrouded-sleep
[/code]

Players should use the Workshop copy only. A second, manually installed copy with the same Mod ID can load old and new files together.

[h2]Configuration[/h2]
All settings are on the Enshrouded Sleep page of the server's sandbox options. The defaults suit most servers.

[b]Core[/b]
- [b]Enabled[/b] (default: on): turns the mod on or off.
- [b]PartialSleepSpeedScale[/b] (default: 1.0, range 0–5): how fast time runs during partial sleep. Below 1.0 is gentler, above 1.0 is faster, and 0 turns the speed-up off.
- [b]AwakePlayerProtectionEnabled[/b] (default: on): keeps awake players' survival needs at the normal rate. Turn it off if you suspect a conflict with another mod that changes hunger, thirst, fatigue, or nutrition.

[b]Sleep notifications (optional)[/b]
- [b]SleepNotificationsEnabled[/b] (default: off): shows players a short banner whenever the sleep state changes, for example [i][Enshrouded Sleep] 1/2 living players sleeping (50%). World time is 20x faster.[/i]

[b]Rested / Well Rested rewards (optional)[/b]
- [b]SleepBenefitsEnabled[/b] (default: off): rewards a good night's sleep with a bonus that lasts for a set number of game hours. The bonus shows as its own moodle; no extra UI mod is needed.
- [b]Rested[/b]: at least 8 hours of sleep gives +10% XP for 4 hours.
- [b]Well Rested[/b]: more than 12 hours gives +10% XP and +10% Endurance recovery for 6 hours.
- The sleep thresholds, durations, and bonus percentages are all adjustable. A new reward replaces the current one rather than stacking, and dying clears it.

[b]Troubleshooting only[/b]
- [b]DiagnosticsEnabled[/b] (default: off): detailed logging for tracking down a problem. It can produce very large logs, so leave it off in normal play.
- [b]DiagnosticForcedCompressionFactor[/b] (default: 1.0): a test tool that only works with diagnostics on and one awake player connected. Keep it at 1.0.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
Mods that change multiplayer sleep, the length of the day, or player hunger, thirst, fatigue, or nutrition may conflict. If players report odd survival stats, turn off [b]AwakePlayerProtectionEnabled[/b] first; the rest of the mod keeps working. If only the banners misbehave, turn off [b]SleepNotificationsEnabled[/b].

